## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates how candidates design end-to-end data-fetch pipelines with planning, dynamic re-planning, and observability in a production analytics context.

## Key Concepts
- LangChain agent workflows and orchestration
- AutoGen planning, tool-use patterns, and multi-agent coordination
- Idempotent REST calls with cache coherence guarantees
- Dynamic re-planning on failures with rollback paths
- Observability: distributed tracing, latency metrics, and freshness validation

## Code Example
```javascript
function buildPlan(input) {
  const planner = new AutoGenPlanner({
    tools: [restTool, cacheTool, loggingTool],
    retryConfig: { maxAttempts: 3, backoffMultiplier: 2 },
    onFailure: 'replan'
  });
  return planner.createPlan({ input, phases: ['fetch', 'validate', 'cache'] });
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

To assess the ability to design policy-aware, scalable agent orchestration that handles multi-tenant data isolation and observability.

## Key Concepts

- LangChain agent orchestration with AutoGen planning
- Private APIs and data catalog integration
- Policy enforcement and tenant isolation
- End-to-end traceability, auditing, and failure handling
- Resilience patterns: retries, backoff, circuit breakers

## Code Example

```javascript
async function plan(request) {
  const { userId, tenantId, dataScope } = request;

  const policyResult = await policyEngine.check({ tenantId, dataScope });
  if (!policyResult.allowed) {
    throw new Error('Access denied: policy violation');
  }

  const catalogResult = await dataCatalog.query({ tenantId, dataScope });
  const apiResult = await privateApi.fetch({ tenantId, dataScope });

  await auditLogger.log({
    tenantId,
    userId,
    steps: [policyResult, catalogResult, apiResult],
    traceId: uuid()
  });

  return { data: apiResult, traceId: traceId };
}
```

This flow ensures tenant-scoped policies are enforced at entry, each component call is traced, and failures are handled with compensating transactions or graceful degradation.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical understanding of a full retrieval pipeline, chunking strategy, and vector stores in a beginner-friendly way applicable to large-prod settings (Lyft/Discord/Adobe).

## Key Concepts

- Token-based chunking and overlap
- Local embeddings vs cloud APIs
- Vector databases and cosine similarity
- Data freshness and refresh strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal in-memory demo (no real model)
const chunks = [
  { text: 'chunk one', vec: [0.1,0.2,0.3] },
  { text: 'chunk two', vec: [0.0,0.2,0.4] }
];
function sim(a,b){ let dot = a.reduce((s,x,i)=> s + x*b[i],0); let na=Math.hypot(...a); let nb=Math.hypot(...b); return dot/(na*nb); }
const q=[0.08,0.18,0.28];
const top = chunks.map((c,i)=>({i, s: sim(c.vec,q)})).sort((A,B)=> B.s-A.s)[0];
console.log(top);
```

